\chapter{Lexical shared and exclusive borrows}
\label{ch:27}
\index{Lexical shared and exclusive borrows}
\index{Borrowing!exclusive}
\index{Borrowing!shared}

A \OnxCodeTerm{borrow T} parameter grants shared access to the referent during a synchronous call. A \OnxCodeTerm{ref T} parameter grants exclusive access along the checked path. Lexically scoped aliases borrow named places, with \OnxCodeTerm{borrow mut} for the exclusive case and \OnxCodeTerm{:=} for the admitted replacement operation. The compiler checks active loans against moves, drops, and other conflicting loans. A borrowed owner payload remains tied to its original owner.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn increment(x: ref i64)
{
  x := x + 1;
}

fn main()
{
  var count = 41;
  {
    let edit = borrow mut count;
    increment(edit);
  }
  assert(count == 42);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

\section{Read a loan conflict}
Consider two aliases rooted at the same named local. Two shared loans can compose in the supported profile, while an overlapping exclusive loan changes the permitted accesses. Evaluate call arguments in their checked order when investigating a conflict. That order can establish a loan before another argument attempts an incompatible access. The C11 lowering may use private typed pointer parameters. This representation does not grant source first-class references, foreign pointer signatures, callbacks, or suspension across a live loan. Try the shared, exclusive, and conflicting versions as separate fixtures.

